## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical on-device multilingual verification, a real-world CRM concern when summaries are generated by LLMs.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-lingual semantic similarity
- Lightweight multilingual models (ONNX/TF-Lite)
- Back-translation checks and drift heuristics
- Privacy-first, on-device inference, staged rollout

## Code Example

```javascript
function fidelity(source, summary, lang) {
  const sim = crossLingualSim(source, summary, lang);
  const back = backTranslate(summary, lang);
  const drift = compare(source, back);
  return { score: combine(sim, drift), explanation: driftReason(drift) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to set fidelity thresholds for different languages?
- How would you evaluate bias or overfitting across languages?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design practical multilingual quality checks that balance latency, privacy, and real-world data issues. It also probes how candidates combine simple heuristics with a small model and how they plan evaluation and rollout.

## Key Concepts
- Multilingual NLP basics and translation quality signals
- Hybrid rule-based plus lightweight ML approaches
- Privacy-preserving inference and latency budgeting
- Offline annotation plans and staged canary rollout

## Code Example
```javascript
function simpleMistranslationFlag(src, trans){
  // naive cue: detect presence/absence of 'not' showing sentiment flip
  const sSrc = /not|n't/.test(src) ? -1 : 1;
  const sTrans = /not|n't/.test(trans) ? -1 : 1;
  return Math.abs(sSrc - sTrans) > 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale to 10+ languages with minimal drift?
- What metrics and alarms would you attach to the canary rollout?

## Why This Is Asked

This angle tests end-to-end system thinking across multilingual NLP, streaming latency, privacy-by-design, RAG, and deployment.

## Key Concepts

- Multilingual intent classifier with adapters trained on English/Spanish/Japanese corpora
- Streaming ASR integration and latency budgets (target ~150 ms)
- Privacy: data minimization, on-device processing where possible, PII masking, tenant isolation
- Retrieval-Augmented Generation for consistent responses
- Deployment: feature flags, drift monitoring, rollback strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code illustrating a multilingual intent classifier with adapters
const { Tokenizer, Model } = require('transformers-js');
const tokenizer = Tokenizer.fromPretrained('xlm-roberta-base');
const model = Model.fromPretrained('xlm-roberta-base', { numLabels: 50 });
model.addAdapter('multilingual');
model.setActiveAdapters(['multilingual']);
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you monitor model drift across languages and handle changes in intents?
- How would you design a safe fallback path when the classifier has low confidence?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a practical, multilingual, on-device NLP component with concrete constraints and a simple evaluation path.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight NER: lexicon + rules, tiny ML fallback
- Multilingual tokenization: EN/ES/DE handling
- Taxonomy normalization and confidence scoring
- On-device privacy and rollout strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
function extractMentions(text, lexicon) {
  const mentions = [];
  for (const [word, label] of Object.entries(lexicon)) {
    if (text.includes(word)) mentions.push({ word, label });
  }
  return mentions;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle ambiguous mentions across languages?
- What metrics and thresholds would you use for the confidence score, and how would you validate them on-device?
